Letting $x=-11$, we get the series:

$\begin{align}
\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{(n+6)(-11+4)^n}{n\cdot7^n}
&=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{(n+6)(-7)^n}{n\cdot7^n}
 \\\\
&=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{(-1)^n\cdot(n+6)\cdot 7^n}{n\cdot7^n} \\\\
&=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty }  \dfrac{(-1)^n\cdot(n+6)}{n} 
\end{align}$

By the $n^{\text{th}}$-term test, we know this series diverges.

Letting $x=3$, we get the series:

$\begin{align}
\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{(n+6)(3+4)^n}{n\cdot7^n}
&=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{(n+6)7^n}{n\cdot7^n}
 \\\\
&=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty }  \dfrac{(n+6)}{n} 
\end{align}$

By the $n^{\text{th}}$-term test, we know this series diverges.
